        "text": "Your crypto wallet also generates an address, which is an alphanumeric identifier that is created based on the public and private keys. In essence, such an address is a specific location on the blockchain records to which tokens can be sent. This means you can share your wallet address with others to receive funds, but you should never disclose your private key to anyone. The private key gives access to your crypto regardless of which wallet you use. Even if your computer gets compromised, you can still access your funds on another device as long as you have the private key. There are many types of crypto wallets. Depending on their working mechanisms, they can be categorized as hot or cold wallets.",
